### UI resolution + per-resolution asset URI substitution.
### See docs/architecture/build-and-tooling.md (icon resolution pipeline) and
### resources/icons/README.md.
###
### ui_resolutions=fhd: app is designed at 1920x1080 design coords; the OS
### auto-downsamples the framebuffer to 720p / 480p on HD/SD devices. For
### 720p this is hardware-verified correct (2026-08-07, issue #419): the
### downsample is supersampling. Declaring hd,fhd instead would disable it
### and require every hardcoded 1920/1080 coordinate to be runtime-computed
### (1920-wide elements overflow the 1280-wide native coord space) for no
### measurable quality win at 720p — that refactor (former tech-debt slug
### hd-native-layout-refactor) was retired; see docs/decisions.md
### (retire-hd-native-layout-refactor). SD/480p output (e.g. CRT displays)
### remains UNVERIFIED on real hardware and may need a different strategy —
### tracked as tech-debt slug sd-resolution-native-support.
###
### uri_resolution_autosub rewrites $$RES$$ in any image URI to sd/hd/fhd at
### load time, keyed off the DEVICE's UI resolution — NOT this manifest's
### design space. 720p devices load the _hd assets even under
### ui_resolutions=fhd (verified via r2d2_bitmaps), decoded at physical
### output size, so the per-resolution PNG triples deliver native-resolution
### bitmaps on HD devices today. Do not remove the _hd assets as "unused".
ui_resolutions=fhd
uri_resolution_autosub=$$RES$$,sd,hd,fhd

